KW  - Metabolic dark matter
KW  - Generative AI
KW  - Self-driving laboratories
AB  - Metabolomics provides the definitive phenotypic readout of biological systems, yet the field remains constrained by the immense complexity of chemical space, facing critical bottlenecks in data mining and the elucidation of "metabolic dark matter." As high-resolution technologies drive a shift toward high-dimensional, spatiotemporally resolved analysis, the resulting data complexity has rapidly outpaced the processing capabilities of traditional bioinformatics. To surmount these challenges, Generative Artificial Intelligence and specifically Large Language Models (LLMs) are driving a paradigm shift, elevating analytical methodologies from passive pattern recognition to proactive and deep semantic cognition. This review systematically explores how LLMs and autonomous AI agents are reshaping the entire metabolomics workflow. Under a unified “science to decision” conceptual framework, we highlight three pivotal translational pillars: (1) knowledge discovery and planning, enabling automated hypothesis generation and intelligent experimental design; (2) data-to-insight translation, focusing on spectral annotation, de novo structural prediction, and dynamic multi-omics reasoning; and (3) decision and autonomous action, integrating AI agents with robotic hardware to establish closed-loop "Self-Driving Laboratories". Finally, we critically evaluate the inherent limitations of these architectures, such as probabilistic hallucinations, physical system drift, and the "signal-to-symbol" gap. To address these challenges, we propose a strategic roadmap toward Native Mass Spectrometry Foundation Models and credible, fully autonomous AI-driven analytical chemistry.
