AB  - We pioneer an inverse-design framework for nanophotonic devices that employs a low-rank adaptation fine-tuned Large Language Model to predict and generate device geometries with both high efficiency and unprecedented generalization in photonic design automation. By adapting a 7 billion-parameter LLama model using low-rank weight updates with only 0.1 % of the parameters trainable, our method simultaneously executes forward prediction and inverse design of a 1:1 power beam splitter on a 220 nm silicon-on-insulator platform with a footprint of 2.5 μm × 2.5 μm. In the forward prediction network, the model achieves a mean transmission Error of −0.0206 dB with a splitting ratio of 1 ± 6 %. In the inverse design network, the generated structures exhibit a mean transmission Error of 0.0044 dB, demonstrating high accuracy and strong generalization across out-of-distribution test sets. The entire fine-tuning process, including 25 epochs for forward prediction and 32 epochs for inverse synthesis, completes in under one hundred hours on a single commercial GPU. To the best of our knowledge, this work represents the first application of Large Language Models to photonics inverse design, paving the way for a new generation of AI-driven methods that enable rapid creation of ultra-compact, high-precision photonic components while significantly reducing computational overhead and expanding the accessible design space.
