AB  - Current few-shot Multimodal Sentiment Analysis (MSA) methods predominantly rely on cross-modal semantic alignment to integrate multimodal data. However, this approach often results in the neglect of modality-specific intrinsic emotional representations and the amplification of inter-modal noise interference. To address these issues, we propose a Dual-path Affective Bridging Network (DABN) for few-shot MSA. Specifically, DABN utilizes a pre-trained Visual-Language Model to reformulate the MSA task as a prompt-based multimodal masked language modeling task, incorporating a cross-modal affective representation space established via pre-training with emotion-annotated images. To further refine feature interaction, we introduce a novel learnable Modality-aware Prompt integration strategy, which effectively enhances both modality-specific representation learning and cross-modal semantic fusion. Finally, to robustly integrate prediction outcomes, we propose a decision-based learnable probability fusion module, named Adaptive Visual-semantic Probability Fusion Evaluator. We validate DABN on two widely used public coarse-grained and three fine-grained MSA datasets, and the experimental results demonstrate that DABN achieves superior performance, outperforming state-of-the-art methods in the few-shot MSA setting, with an average improvement of 1.13% in Accuracy and 1.50% in F1 score. The code is available at https://github.com/JingVIPLab/DABN.
