AB  - Maritime shipping accounts for approximately 80% of global trade, but accident risk remains high owing to the complex operating environment. Current Bayesian network (BN)-based maritime accident risk analysis faces three challenges: subjective identification of risk-influencing factors (RIFs), expert-dependent network construction, and costly annotated data for parameter learning. To address these challenges, this study proposes an integrated framework that synergizes Large Language Model (LLM) semantic capabilities with data-driven causal analysis to construct maritime accident BNs directly from unstructured reports. The framework integrates three core innovations: (1) automated RIF taxonomy construction via LLM-based semantic extraction and unsupervised clustering; (2) objective structure learning by generating directed acyclic graphs from extracted causal chains using a hybrid DEMATEL-ISM approach, thereby eliminating reliance on subjective questionnaires; (3) efficient parameter estimation leveraging optimized prompt engineering and multi-run voting strategies to infer conditional probabilities in few-shot settings. Validated through a case study of 566 Chinese maritime accident reports, the framework identified 28 standardized RIFs and established a hierarchical causal model, revealing the most critical proximate, root causes and their relations. By transforming unstructured accident narratives into an interpretable inference engine, this study offers a robust methodology for shifting maritime safety management from post-event description to proactive, data-driven analysis.
