UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950705126016564
KW  - Poisoning attacks defense
KW  - Network security
KW  - Incremental update
KW  - Counterfactual computation
AB  - In recent years, with the rapid development of large language models, the threat of poisoning attacks has become increasingly severe, as even a small proportion of malicious data can significantly alter a model’s behavior. Although many defense methods have been proposed, each has its own limitations. In this work, we propose Counterfactual Incremental Defense against Poisoning Attacks (CIDPA), which combines counterfactual metrics with incremental updates to achieve a continuous, robust defense. Specifically, we compute the minimal counterfactual cost to estimate, for each new sample, the smallest feasible change required to flip the model’s current prediction. The model is then updated progressively using a sliding window mechanism. Because counterfactual costs typically exhibit relatively stable statistical patterns under clean conditions, CIDPA monitors window-level distributional statistics to detect potential contamination. When the window exhibits normal or mild poisoning, the system operates in the within-window mode, where counterfactual metrics are standardized and analyzed locally to filter subtle anomalies. However, when the degree of poisoning becomes severe and the window distribution is no longer reliable, CIDPA automatically switches to cross-window mode, using statistical patterns from clean historical windows as a reference baseline. This switching mechanism prevents the model from self-contamination and ensures stability even under large-scale poisoning. In the experiments, we intentionally manipulated the datasets to simulate three different poisoning attack scenarios. Across varying proportions of poisoned data, our proposed CIDPA framework consistently achieved strong defense performance and outperformed two existing poisoning defense methods in most evaluated settings.
