AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have increasingly become the cognitive core of AI agents, enabling these systems to perceive environments, reason about goals, and execute actions. This shift has established LLM-based agents as a promising paradigm for building adaptive and autonomous AI systems. However, deploying such agents in open, dynamic, and long-horizon environments remains constrained by insufficient grounding, inefficient reasoning, tool hallucination, and security risks associated with persistent memory stores. Existing surveys have examined key agent modules, but their module-centric framing leaves cross-stage coordination within the agent lifecycle underexplored. To address this gap, this survey proposes a lifecycle-oriented framework that treats LLM-based agents as iterative systems rather than as collections of isolated functional modules. Within this framework, we position graph-augmented LLM-based agents (GLAs) as a structure-aware extension of LLM-based agents. In GLAs, explicit relational representations serve as cognitive scaffolds for tool orchestration, memory organization and retrieval, and long-horizon planning. We further operationalize this lifecycle framework by reviewing implementation frameworks, graphical user interface (GUI) agent applications, and evaluation benchmarks for tool use, memory, and planning. Finally, we identify reliability, efficiency, persistent-memory security, and scalability as key challenges in advancing LLM-based agents toward multi-agent ecosystems and embodied intelligence.
