AB  - The rapid growth of online video content has increased the demand for accurate text-video retrieval. However, information asymmetry between rich video content and brief textual queries hinders effective cross-modal alignment. To address this challenge, we propose KAViC (Knowledge-Guided Augmentation and Video-Caption Interaction), a unified framework that enhances text-video retrieval from both data and architectural perspectives. From a data-centric perspective, we introduce a dual-stage textual augmentation strategy. During training, a multimodal large language model generates detailed segment-level captions to enhance semantic supervision. During inference, a large language model leverages structured concepts retrieved from a lightweight dataset-specific knowledge graph to expand short queries into diverse yet semantically grounded variants. From an architectural perspective, we design a video-caption interaction module integrated with an adaptive weighting and filtering mechanism to model the alignment between video features and generated captions, enabling fine-grained cross-modal reasoning. By improving both semantic richness and cross-modal interaction, KAViC effectively alleviates information asymmetry. Experiments on standard benchmarks demonstrate that our method achieves consistent and significant gains over strong baselines.
