AB  - Textual disclosures enable scalable, cross-industry measurement of corporate digital activities. Generative Large Language Models (GLLMs) offer contextual understanding, yet prompts introduce subjectivity. We develop a prompt-free GLLM framework to classify digital-related content in Management Discussion and Analysis (MD&A) sections of annual reports. To ensure measurement reliability, we evaluate two strategies to adapt pre-trained GLLMs for classification: domain-adaptive pre-training (DAPT) to address potential vocabulary gaps, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) to reduce prompt sensitivity. We benchmark these post-training strategies against a BERT baseline using labeled MD&A sentences. Results show that Base-GLLM already encodes sufficient digital vocabulary, rendering DAPT gains marginal. In contrast, PEFT yields substantial improvements and enables prompt-free inference, mitigating prompt subjectivity. The fine-tuned model remains stable under class-imbalance conditions and across firm, industry, and temporal splits. Applying the fine-tuned classifier, we construct a disclosure-based proxy for digital activities. To mitigate cheap-talk concerns, we validate this proxy against observable indicators including digital recruitment and patents. Associations are significant and directionally consistent across policy-shock and matching-based comparability checks. Overall, we provide a prompt-free framework for constructing and validating a disclosure-based measurement artifact of corporate digital activities, enabling objective and reproducible measurement at scale.
