AB  - Reinforcement-learning traffic signal control performs well in simulation but remains brittle to incidents, demand surges, detector failures, and topology changes; fixed rewards cannot adapt priorities. We present ResiLight, a hierarchical framework combining a physics-informed graph multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) controller, a large language model (LLM) supervisor, and a rule-based safety shield. A parameter-shared graph-attention double deep Q-network is regularized toward a max-pressure prior. Every minute, an open-weight LLM maps a network digest to one of five operating modes, which select among behaviors learned offline under mode-specific rewards; constrained decoding, validation, and shielded max-pressure fallback bound supervisory failures. Simulation experiments cover a synthetic grid and three real networks with ten seeds. Under recurrent demand, ResiLight reduces average travel time by 2.9–4.6% relative to the strongest of ten baselines, a reimplemented ENB-RL. Under lane closures, demand surges, and detector failures, its degradation is 11.9–15.8 percentage points lower than that of ENB-RL on all three real networks, and 4.9–6.5 points lower than mode-conditioned ENB-RL on Ingolstadt21; under emergency-vehicle priority, which raises travel time by at most 5.4% for any reported method, the margin is below one point. Grid-trained policies transfer to the real networks with 4.2–4.5% loss, versus 6.1–22.6% for the learned baselines. Ablations attribute transferability mainly to physics regularization and robustness mainly to mode conditioning. The LLM supervisor performs on par with the best classifier trained on labeled multi-network digests without requiring such data, and the shield enforces timing and sequencing constraints without a statistically significant travel-time cost.
