AB  - The increasing digitalization and interconnectivity of railway Operational Technology (OT) systems have significantly expanded the attack surface that operators must defend. Signaling controllers, SCADA platforms, and train control systems, once air-gapped, now share infrastructure with enterprise IT, cloud services, and remote-access gateways. These structural changes expose safetycritical systems to threat actors whose capabilities continue to evolve faster than many standard-based threat-modeling practices used in current railway operations. Traditional threat modeling methodologies, such as STRIDE and PASTA, are largely static and reliant on pre-defined threat taxonomies, rendering them inadequate for capturing the dynamic and context-specific risks inherent to modern railway environments. This paper proposes the AI-enhanced Rail Virtual Twin (AI-RVT), a conceptual threat modeling framework that combines a high-fidelity virtual replica of a railway system with an AI/LLM engine grounded through Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and a Threat Knowledge Graph (TKG). Operating entirely in a synthetic environment, the framework is designed to enable adaptive threat modeling that maps attack vectors to the MITRE ATT&CK for ICS taxonomy and generates explainable risk assessments aligned with IEC 62443 and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework. We illustrate the architecture through a conceptual zero-day attack scenario on a CBTC/FRMCS signaling system.
