AB  - The final chapter is an overview of artificial intelligence (AI) as it relates to accessibility the future of inclusion. It begins with an explanation of how large language models are trained according to patterns and how those patterns are subject to systemic bias and faulty intelligence. The chapter examines the ethical modeling of statistical data, demonstrating how software can learn to discriminate on the basis of disability, race, gender, ethnicity, and other protected classes. The chapter reviews the legal impact of AI bias on people with disabilities, followed by a comparison of various AI tools that generate alt text for images while exploring the social and historical context of image descriptions. The chapter then covers AI-enabled solutions created by people with disabilities, highlighting the importance of empathy and the harmful effects when AI does not account for the lived experience of disability. After an examination of the controversy surrounding intelligent social agents (ISAs), the chapter explores the use of brain–computer interfaces (BCIs) as a means of training AI datasets. The chapter concludes by bookending themes introduced at the beginning of the book—that despite stereotypes and stigma, we are all beneficiaries of inclusive design and will oneday reap the rewards of accessibility.
