AB  - Large language models (LLMs) encounter significant challenges in multi-step mathematical reasoning, primarily due to error propagation across reasoning chains and optimization instability during preference learning. To address these limitations, we propose a dynamic self-supervised training framework with Fine-Grained Reasoning Preference Optimization (FGRPO), a fine-grained training approach that optimizes individual reasoning steps according to human preferences, coupled with an Iterative Strategy Optimization Model (IOSM) that employs an Exponential Moving Average (EMA) mechanism to enhance the stability of self-generated preference learning. We evaluated our methodology on three prominent open source models—Mistral-7B, Llama3-8B, and Qwen2-7B—across four challenging mathematical reasoning datasets: MATH, GSM8K, SVAMP, and AQuA-RAT. Our results demonstrate that FGRPO achieves substantial improvements in reasoning accuracy, with gains ranging from 1.6% to 3.3%. When integrated with IOSM, the approach yields consistent performance enhancements exceeding 0.6% per iteration on the MATH dataset. Through the analysis of conditional dependencies between reasoning steps, we observe that the ratio of correct to incorrect steps converges toward 1:1, ensuring that the model reduces errors while preserving reasoning integrity without missing critical steps. Our findings establish a scalable and noise-resistant training paradigm that significantly strengthens the mathematical reasoning capabilities of LLMs, reducing the performance gap with state-of-the-art commercial models.
