AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) can perform rhetorical move-step analysis, yet their probabilistic architecture means repeated runs may produce different annotations from identical inputs, a direct threat to research reproducibility. While prior studies have focused primarily on classification accuracy, the conditions for consistent replication remain understudied. To systematically isolate the effect of sampling temperature on LLM reliability, this study generated over 450,000 sentence annotations across 18,000 runs (50 iterations × 40 articles × 9 temperature settings) using GPT-4.1-mini on the CaRS-50 corpus (Lam & Nnamoko, 2025). The central finding is a decoupling of accuracy and consistency. Classification performance remained stable across all temperature settings (Move-level F1 ∼0.86; Step-level F1 ∼0.46), but annotation consistency degraded substantially as temperature increased. At low temperatures (T ≤ 0.2), inter-iteration reliability was excellent (Krippendorff's α > 0.94). At T = 2.0, many step-level annotations fell below acceptable thresholds (α < 0.67) and over 50 % of all outputs failed structurally, becoming completely unparsable. These results demonstrate that a single accurate run does not guarantee a reproducible one, and that lower temperatures optimize reliability without sacrificing performance. To support methodological rigor in automated discourse analysis, practical guidelines are offered for pilot testing inter-iteration reliability, transparently reporting parameter settings, and adopting open-weight models to preserve parameter control.
