KW  - Reinforcement learning
KW  - Machine translation
KW  - Dynamic head gating
KW  - Pedagogical policy network
KW  - Language-specific attention heads
AB  - Multilingual large language models (LLMs) suffer from suboptimal and fixed allocations of attention head importance across diverse linguistic tasks, especially translation. Current static pruning or scaling approaches lack adaptability to specific language pairs or task contexts. To overcome this limitation, we introduce the Translation-Aware Language Instruction System (TALIS) – a novel closed-loop framework that dynamically modulates attention heads for enhanced multilingual translation. TALIS reframes the problem as an optimization-driven pedagogical cycle, integrating three core components: (1) a Translation-Aware Diagnosis Module that evaluates head importance via task-specific gradients, (2) a Learner State Encoder that monitors model performance, and (3) a Pedagogical Policy Network trained with reinforcement learning to maximize translation reward (BLEU and COMET) through adaptive head gating. Extensive experiments on models such as Aya-23, Llama-3.2, and Mistral-7B validate TALIS’s efficacy. On the Flores-101 benchmark, TALIS achieves an average gain of +2.57 BLEU over vanilla models and +1.11 BLEU over static head-masking baselines on Aya-23-8B, averaged over ten language pairs (three independent runs), with consistent COMET improvements. Ablation studies underscore the contribution of each component. This work pioneers a dynamic, goal-oriented paradigm for modulating multilingual LLMs, shifting from static analysis toward intelligent, task-aware capacity allocation.
