AB  - Machine unlearning, a technique for removing specific knowledge from trained models, has recently attracted increasing attention, particularly in CLIP-based frameworks. While existing methods can effectively eliminate predefined target concepts, they often leave behind residual knowledge in the form of semantically synonymous expressions, enabling potential reconstruction of the forgotten concept. In this paper, we address the challenge of robust concept unlearning in CLIP, aiming to eliminate not only explicit target concepts but also their synonymous variants. To this end, we propose a novel unlearning framework named RUCLIP. Central to our approach is the introduction of semantic anchors, comprising direct, indirect, and transfer anchors. And we develop a two-stage generate-and-rank strategy to construct them. These anchors guide the fine-tuning of CLIP under dual objectives: suppressing target semantics while preserving unrelated knowledge. RUCLIP achieves a forgetting accuracy of 1.67 % on unseen synonyms on CIFAR-100, while attaining a recall of only 1.45 % in image retrieval. Both results are substantially better than those of the existing state-of-the-art baseline CLIPErase, which achieves 47 % forgetting accuracy and 7.47 % recall. Extensive experiments on image classification and cross-modal retrieval on CIFAR-100 and ImageNet-1k demonstrate that our method significantly enhances CLIP’s robustness to synonymous prompts, without degrading its performance to unrelated concepts.
